{
  "id": "house-robber",
  "questionId": "house-robber",
  "slug": "house-robber",
  "title": "House Robber",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Dp 1d",
  "patterns": [
    "dynamic programming"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75",
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "at house i, you have exactly two choices: rob it, or don't - what does each choice cost you in terms of which earlier houses are still usable?",
    "if you rob house i, you get nums[i] plus the best result up through house i-2, since i-1 is now off limits",
    "if you skip house i, your best result is just whatever the best was through house i-1",
    "best(i) = max(best(i-1), best(i-2) + nums[i]) - roll just the last two best values forward instead of storing a full array"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "treating this like Maximum Subarray and forgetting the 'no two adjacent' constraint entirely",
    "off-by-one on the base cases for the first one or two houses",
    "recomputing best(i-2) from scratch instead of tracking it alongside best(i-1)"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You plan to rob houses along a street, where `nums[i]` is the amount of\ncash stored in house `i`. Every house is wired to a security system that\nalerts the police if two adjacent houses are both robbed on the same\nnight, so no two houses you rob can be next to each other.\n\n**Return the maximum total amount you can rob.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 100",
      "0 <= nums[i] <= 400"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single house (rob it)",
      "two houses (rob the larger one)",
      "all houses hold the same amount"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
